SUPPLY CHAIN MORNING - THE WEEK AHEAD
Sunday, March 8, 2026

Clocks went forward overnight. If your dock schedules run on a system that does not know that, today is the day you find out. What we are watching this week.

Monday: The National Retail Federation's monthly port tracker. The trade group has been forecasting a soft first half; watch whether they revise March up after the TPM chatter.

Tuesday: A Senate hearing on the proposed port-fee structure for foreign-built vessels. The ocean carriers have said they would pass the fees through as a surcharge; the retailers have said they would rather the fees not exist. Expect noise, not law.

Wednesday: February CPI. The goods component has been flat; the freight and warehousing components inside services are what we read.

Thursday: The big Chicago industrial real-estate conference. Warehouse vacancy in the top 20 markets sits at 7.1%, up from 3.3% at the 2022 trough, and new construction starts are at a decade low. Landlords will say the bottom is in. Tenants will not.

Friday: Preliminary March consumer sentiment, and the deadline for comments on the STB's proposed data-reporting rule for first-mile/last-mile rail service.

Also: a Canadian rail labor deadline on Wednesday that has not gotten enough attention, and a produce-industry conference in Monterey where the traceability-rule delay will be the only topic.

See you tomorrow at 6, Pacific Daylight Time.

